<main class="page">
<!-- HERO -->
<header class="hero">
<div class="left">
<span class="label accent">Design leadership at Tive • 3rd Quarter 2026</span>
<h1>Scaling design judgment, not just design output</h1>
<p class="lede">Evolving a senior product design team from reactive execution toward an autonomous, strategic function.</p>
<p class="ctx">Tive is a global supply chain visibility platform, growing rapidly as a Series C company.</p>
</div>
<div class="stats" role="list">
<div class="stat" role="listitem"><b class="word">Web · Mobile · Hardware</b><span>Cross-platform design leadership</span></div>
<div class="stat" role="listitem"><b>4</b><span>Key product areas</span></div>
<div class="stat" role="listitem"><b class="word">Player / coach</b><span>Management + hands-on systems work</span></div>
<div class="stat" role="listitem"><b class="word">1 hr → 10 min</b><span>Prep time per design review</span></div>
</div>
</header>
<!-- AT A GLANCE -->
<section id="glance">
<div class="glance">
<div><span class="label">Context</span><p>Enterprise software spanning web, native mobile, and connected hardware, while the engineering team was growing quickly and adopting AI.</p></div>
<div><span class="label">My role</span><p>Product Design Manager and player-coach: people management, design direction, design operations, and hands-on systems work. I partner with product managers, engineering managers, and product leadership.</p></div>
<div><span class="label">The problem</span><p>Product and engineering were increasingly &ldquo;waiting on design.&rdquo; A stretched senior team needed a repeatable way to focus on strategic work and let others make good design decisions.</p></div>
<div><span class="label">What I changed</span><p>A lightweight operating model for visibility and ownership, an AI-assisted review-prep workflow, and a risk-based framework for how much evidence each decision needs.</p></div>
<div><span class="label">What happened</span><p>&ldquo;Waiting on design&rdquo; is no longer something we hear. Designers moved from reactive product partners to proactive design leaders, driving architectural changes like the tables and global navigation redesigns, and review prep dropped from about an hour to about ten minutes.</p></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- THE MOMENT -->
<section id="challenge">
<div class="two">
<div class="sec-head">
<span class="label">The leadership challenge</span>
<h2>A senior team shouldn't require a manager in every decision.</h2>
</div>
<div class="prose">
<p>The feedback kept coming from engineering and product: &ldquo;we&rsquo;re waiting on design.&rdquo; It got louder as the engineering team grew and adopted AI. We were launching faster, with less design confidence and more pressure on the team, and we were stretched thin trying to be in more places at once.</p>
<p>The real problem wasn&rsquo;t design capacity. We were spending our time on tedious, tactical work, and we had no repeatable structure for raising the bar on strategic work or for helping others make good design decisions. Not every outcome needed a Figma mock.</p>
<p>We needed to be less reactive and more proactive to keep a high bar for UX while reducing design debt.</p>
<p>The goal wasn't to add process or to be a decision gatekeeper; it was to create <i>enough</i> structure and context that designers could operate more <b>independently</b> while raising the <b>quality and consistency</b> of the work.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="compare">
<div aria-label="Before: a linear hand-off" class="flow before">
<span class="label">Before</span>
<div class="node">Product requirements</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node">Design</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node">Mocks</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node">Engineering</div>
</div>
<div aria-label="Direction: shared context and designer ownership" class="flow">
<span class="label accent">Direction</span>
<div class="node">Product strategy</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="triad"><div class="node">Product</div><i>↔</i><div class="node strong">Design</div><i>↔</i><div class="node">Eng</div></div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node">Shared context</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node accent">Designer ownership</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node">Evidence + critique</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node">Execution</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="thesis">
<p>The question became: <span class="q">how do I scale design judgment without becoming the bottleneck?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- RESULTS -->
<section id="outcomes">
<div class="sec-head">
<span class="label">Results</span>
<h2>What changed</h2>
</div>
<div class="outcomes">
<div><span class="label accent">Team</span><h3>More ownership</h3><p>Senior designers engaging earlier, driving alignment, and operating with greater autonomy.</p><p class="proof">Designers have led architectural changes, including the tables and global navigation redesigns.</p></div>
<div><span class="label accent">Quality</span><h3>Better judgment</h3><p>More informed critique, intentional validation, and more attention to shared patterns.</p><p class="proof">Mobile app architecture is now scaled strategically instead of by hunch, with shorter time to launch and more effective launches.</p></div>
<div><span class="label accent">Leverage</span><h3>Less coordination overhead</h3><p>AI and lightweight operations make context, feedback, and cross-team awareness easier to maintain.</p><p class="proof">Review prep dropped from about an hour to about ten minutes, across roughly 20 reviews.</p></div>
</div>
<blockquote class="quote"><span class="ph">[Quote from a designer, PM, or EM about working this way]</span><cite><span class="ph">[Role, anonymized]</span></cite></blockquote>
</section>
<!-- HOW I DID IT -->
<section id="model">
<div class="sec-head">
<span class="label">How I did it</span>
<h2>Three places where better systems created leverage.</h2>
</div>
<div class="four three">
<a href="#team"><span class="n">01 · TEAM</span><h3>Scale the team</h3><p>Create autonomy without losing alignment.</p><span class="go">Operating model ↓</span></a>
<a href="#manager"><span class="n">02 · MANAGER</span><h3>Scale the manager</h3><p>Use AI to raise the quality of feedback.</p><span class="go">Review prep ↓</span></a>
<a href="#judgment"><span class="n">03 · JUDGMENT</span><h3>Scale judgment</h3><p>Match design rigor to actual product risk.</p><span class="go">Risk framework ↓</span></a>
</div>
</section>
<!-- 01 TEAM -->
<section id="team">
<div class="iv-head">
<span class="n">01</span>
<div class="t"><span class="label">Scale the team</span><h2>Autonomy requires <i>visibility</i>, not more <i>approval gates</i>.</h2></div>
</div>
<div class="two">
<div class="problem">
<span class="label">Problem</span>
<p>Designers needed room to own their work, while the team still needed visibility into shared patterns, conflicting initiatives, upcoming needs, and chances to collaborate.<br><br>I introduced Design Triage: a space to align on our shared work and pull in design system and architecture alongside our feature work.</p>
<figure class="board">
<button aria-label="Enlarge the design board screenshot" class="board-btn" type="button"><img alt="The design team's Jira backlog: a quick filter shows 9 of 25 items, each a Design System Request, such as Accordion pattern, Alert status color semantics, and Updated Table Pagination Pattern." height="512" src="data:image/png;base64," width="794"><span aria-hidden="true" class="board-zoom">⤢ Enlarge</span></button>
<figcaption><span aria-hidden="true" class="lnk"></span><span class="cap">The <b>DS / patterns</b> lane, live: 9 of 25 backlog items are design-system requests, queued for Triage alongside feature work.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div>
<div class="panel scroll-x">
<div aria-label="Team operating model" class="ops">
<div class="node">Upcoming work</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node strong">Design triage</div>
<div class="lanes">
<div class="node">Product initiatives</div>
<div class="node linked">DS / patterns</div>
<div class="node">UX papercuts</div>
</div>
<div class="join"></div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node accent">Designer ownership</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node">Jam · critique · alignment</div><div class="arrow"></div>
<div class="node">Development</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="callouts">
<div><h3>Design Triage</h3><p>Weekly visibility into upcoming work, conflicts, risks, and opportunities.</p></div>
<div><h3>Async coordination</h3><p>Less meeting overhead; better awareness of related work, AI-assistance where beneficial.</p></div>
<div><h3>Design Jam</h3><p>Earlier collaboration, including PMs when useful. Documented decisions and shared notes.</p></div>
<div><h3>Open design channel</h3><p>Broader access to the design team and to feedback. Quicker ideas into tickets.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="result">
<span class="label">Result</span>
<p>1. Designers gained room to drive bigger architectural changes in support of the product roadmap, including the tables and global navigation redesigns.<br><br>2. I gained earlier visibility into the decisions where my involvement had the most impact.</p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- 02 MANAGER -->
<section id="manager">
<div class="iv-head">
<span class="n">02</span>
<div class="t"><span class="label">Scale the manager</span><h2>AI helped me spend less time reconstructing context and more time challenging the work.</h2></div>
</div>
<div class="two">
<div class="problem">
<span class="label">Problem</span>
<p>Managing several senior designers across simultaneous initiatives created an unexpected constraint: <i>useful feedback required reconstructing a lot of context before every review</i>.</p>
<p>So I built a review-prep workflow. Give it a project name, it gathers recent context across Slack, Drive, and Figma and structures it around the questions I need answered before a critique.</p>
</div>
<div class="panel scroll-x">
<div aria-label="AI design review prep workflow" class="prep">
<div class="sources"><div class="node">Slack</div><div class="node">Drive</div><div class="node">Figma</div></div>
<div class="wire"></div>
<div class="node strong">AI review<br>prep</div>
<div class="wire"></div>
<div class="qs">
<div class="node">Why this solution?</div>
<div class="node">Alternatives explored?</div>
<div class="node">Tradeoffs?</div>
<div class="node">Open questions?</div>
<div class="node accent out">→ Design critique</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<figure class="board prepshot">
<button aria-label="Enlarge the review prep screenshot" class="board-btn" type="button"><img alt="Excerpt from an AI-generated design review prep brief: a Biggest unanswered questions section listing open decisions about scope, architecture, edge cases, carried-over scope, and QA ownership." height="550" src="data:image/png;base64," width="946"><span aria-hidden="true" class="board-zoom">⤢ Enlarge</span></button>
<figcaption><span class="cap">From a review-prep brief: the open questions worth challenging, gathered before the critique starts.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="result">
<span class="label">Result</span>
<p>1. Faster context and fewer basic questions, so reviews go straight to better feedback. Prep dropped from about an hour to about ten minutes across roughly 20 reviews.<br><br>2. AI didn't replace design judgment. It created more room for it.</p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- 03 JUDGMENT -->
<section id="judgment">
<div class="iv-head">
<span class="n">03</span>
<div class="t"><span class="label">Scale judgment</span><h2>Not every design problem deserves the same process.</h2></div>
</div>
<div class="two">
<div class="problem">
<span class="label">System</span>
<p>Risk scoring, a library of validation methods—heuristic review, analytics, live in-product validation, surveys, interviews, usability testing—discussion in Design Triage, and an AI-supported analysis tool open to the broader org.</p>
</div>
<div class="panel scroll-x">
<div aria-label="Validation by impact and uncertainty" class="matrix" role="table">
<div class="ax" role="columnheader"></div>
<div class="ax col" role="columnheader">Low uncertainty</div>
<div class="ax col" role="columnheader">High uncertainty</div>
<div class="ax" role="rowheader">Low impact</div>
<div class="cell c1" role="cell"><small>Evidence: light</small><b>Ship + observe</b></div>
<div class="cell c2" role="cell"><small>Evidence: some</small><b>Lightweight validation</b></div>
<div class="ax" role="rowheader">High impact</div>
<div class="cell c3" role="cell"><small>Evidence: focused</small><b>Targeted validation</b></div>
<div class="cell c4" role="cell"><small>Evidence: deep</small><b>Research + deeper validation</b></div>
</div>
<div class="steps">
<span class="node">Assess risk</span><i>→</i><span class="node">Choose method</span><i>→</i><span class="node">Gather evidence</span><i>→</i><span class="node accent">Decide</span>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="principle">
<p>Rather than enforce one "design process," I wanted designers to share a framework for deciding how much evidence a decision required.</p>
</div>
<figure class="board manual">
<button aria-label="Enlarge the validation methods screenshot" class="board-btn" type="button"><img alt="Excerpt from the Design Operations Manual listing validation methods: heuristic or expert review, analytics and behavioral review, Pendo live validation, survey, and user interview, each with what it answers, when to use it, and examples." height="991" src="data:image/png;base64," width="800"><span aria-hidden="true" class="board-zoom">⤢ Enlarge</span></button>
<figcaption><span class="cap">Validation methods as listed in the Design Operations Manual</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="result">
<span class="label">Result</span>
<p>Mobile app architecture decisions are now made strategically rather than by hunch, which has shortened time to launch and made launches more effective.</p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- THE PEOPLE SIDE -->
<section id="people">
<div class="sec-head">
<span class="label">The people side</span>
<h2>A system only works if the designers grow with it.</h2>
</div>
<div class="outcomes">
<div><span class="label accent">Ownership</span><h3>Bigger scope</h3><p><span class="ph">[Example: a designer who took on bigger scope or led a cross-team decision, and what changed for them]</span></p></div>
<div><span class="label accent">Coaching</span><h3>How I coach</h3><p><span class="ph">[Your 1:1 cadence, how you critique senior work, and one concrete example of feedback that landed]</span></p></div>
<div><span class="label accent">Team</span><h3>Hiring and structure</h3><p><span class="ph">[Any hiring, org-design, or role-clarity changes, or one hard conversation, generalized]</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- FAILED -->
<section id="failed">
<div class="sec-head">
<span class="label">One experiment that failed</span>
<h2>Scaling also meant knowing when to remove process.</h2>
</div>
<div class="killed">
<div><span class="label">Experiment</span><h3>Design Brief v1</h3><p>A running artifact meant to preserve design goals and decisions throughout an initiative.</p></div>
<div><span class="label">What happened</span><ul><li>Hard to maintain</li><li>Poor discoverability</li><li>Didn't solve a big enough problem</li><li>Added process when it wasn't needed</li></ul></div>
<div><span class="label">Decision</span><span class="stamp">No Go</span></div>
<div><span class="label accent">Going Forward</span><p>PM-designer-EM collaboration serves us better instead of creating another designer-maintained artifact.</p></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- LEARNED -->
<section id="learned">
<div class="learned">
<div class="sec-head"><span class="label">What I learned</span><h2>The manager shouldn't be <span>the system.</span></h2></div>
<div class="prose">
<p>The biggest shift in my management approach was realizing that scaling a senior team isn't about getting better at keeping track of everything. It's about building systems that <b>make my involvement less necessary</b>.</p>
<p>Clear ownership creates autonomy. Shared frameworks distribute judgment. AI reduces the cost of context. And good design architecture lets individual decisions compound into something larger.</p>
<p>My role increasingly became finding where the team was losing leverage and designing the conditions that let talented designers do better work without waiting for me.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- ALSO: PRODUCT -->
<section id="product">
<div class="iv-head">
<span class="n">+</span>
<div class="t"><span class="label">Also: scaling the product</span><h2>Some design problems shouldn't be solved one feature at a time.</h2></div>
</div>
<div class="obs">
<div class="problem">
<span class="label">Observation</span>
<p>We kept displaying the same underlying product objects in very different contexts. Solving each instance independently created inconsistency and lengthy, repeated design work.</p>
<p>So we defined each core object once—with shared hierarchy and interaction rules—and let context set the density. <em class="hl">Carrier and Driver</em> were the first to be trialed in Pickup Verification and Commodities work.</p>
</div>
</div>
